  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Friday)\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT SAT AUG 29 2026\n\nThe short-term weather pattern across Central Alabama continues\nto be defined by a slow-moving upper-level shortwave trough\nmeandering along the Gulf Coast. At the surface, a prevailing\nnortherly flow is facilitating the steady advection of a drier\nand slightly cooler airmass into the northern portions of the\nforecast area. This influx of lower dewpoints is effectively\nlimiting moisture availability across the far north, keeping the\nfocus of any diurnal convective activity strictly confined to the\nsouthern and central counties over the next 12 to 36 hours.\nWhile the overall severe weather threat remains low due to weak\nvertical wind shear, atmospheric conditions will still support\nlocalized convective development where moisture remains deeper.\nShowers are already developing along a convergence zone\nassociated with the shortwave energy to our south. Look for the\nconvergence zone to be between I-59 and I-85 today and into the\nevening hours.\n\nA significant pattern transition is expected as we move into\nearly next week. Computer model guidance is in better agreement\nregarding the building of a broad, high-amplitude upper-level\nridge. This high-pressure system is projected to shift from the\nwestern states toward the east, establishing a significant axis\nstretching from Arkansas to the Carolinas. This building ridge\nwill act to shear out the stubborn Gulf Coast low and steer the\nremaining shortwave energy westward toward the Mississippi River.\nAs the ridge establishes dominance over the regional weather, it\nwill create a subsidence cap that effectively suppresses daily\nconvection across the entire forecast area, although isolated\nafternoon showers will still remain plausible..\n\nWith the suppression of rain chances to levels below\nclimatological normals, temperatures will steadily climb back\nwell into the 90s. The combination of building heat and lingering\nsurface moisture will likely necessitate another round of Heat\nAdvisories. Current trends suggest that these heat products will\nbecome increasingly likely starting around Tuesday or Wednesday\nof next week as the ridge axis centers itself over the Deep\nSouth.\n\n16",
